I've wired the green validation door to record the git unhittable reading. Build, the full cargo test run, clippy with -D warnings and cargo fmt --check all pass. The work is in four commits (three WIP code/test commits plus the ARCHITECTURE entry, as the ordering rule required). The final commit had nothing left to add, so no commit carries the "Day 214 … (Task 1)" title.

What changed
• **src/commands_risk_snapshots.rs:** record_green_validation_to used to pass None, None, None. It now runs the git existence check and saves git_born_after / git_unmeasured.
  • It uses the same probe as the watch path; I didn't write a second git check.
  • The two ledger-join fields are still left out, because no snapshot timestamp is available here. The comment now says that reason covers only the ledger join.
  • If the snapshot hash doesn't resolve, the files are counted as unmeasured, never as a 0.
  • The `trigger: "cli" and severity: "watch_success"` strings are unchanged.
• **`src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs:** git_born_after_at now shares its logic with a new git_born_after_in_dir`, which can run against a chosen repo. Existing behaviour is the same.
• **Signature change:** `record_green_validation_to` takes a new `repo_dir` argument so tests can point it at a temp repo. The one real caller in `src/commands_risk.rs` passes ".". That file wasn't in the task's Files list.

Tests (green_door_git_reading_tests in commands_risk_unhittable_tests.rs). Each test makes a real temp git repo, calls the real record_green_validation_to, and reads the row back with the real parser. No expected values are typed in.
• A file missing at the snapshot counts git_born_after = 1, and a file that was present isn't counted.
• When every file existed at the snapshot, the row carries an explicit 0.
• With a hash that doesn't resolve, both files count as unmeasured, not as born after.

